        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-59c7df44837fae36\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Armand’s “Si vous saviez comme je vous aime!” is principally an avowal presenting the intensity of his love for Marguerite as true. Although it occurs during courtship and contributes to their negotiation of a liaison, the words themselves do not undertake a commitment; explicit promises follow separately. The low voice, physical intimacy, excitement, and exclamation mark indicate passionate delivery but do not independently make the utterance an expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Armand directly addresses Marguerite in dialogue, as recounted by the first-person narrator.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Armand has already claimed that nobody has loved Marguerite as he does and has described his love as lasting three years. The highlighted utterance reiterates and intensifies that avowal while he embraces her. Marguerite treats it as a sincerity claim—“Bien vrai?”—and then makes any prospective relationship conditional on his obedience and discretion. Those negotiations and Armand’s subsequent promises supply the scene’s commitment dimension, but they do not turn this core utterance itself into an undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Si vous saviez comme je vous aime! lui disais-je tout bas.”\",\"supports\":\"The construction explicitly presents the degree of Armand’s loving state as true; “tout bas” marks delivery without establishing expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Armand says, “personne ne vous a jamais aimée comme je vous aime,” and explains that his love began three years before.\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted words continue an extended, deliberate account of an enduring emotional state, strongly supporting T rather than a reflex-like production.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite replies, “Bien vrai?” and Armand answers, “Je vous jure.”\",\"supports\":\"Her response tests the truth and sincerity of his avowal; the oath is a separate reinforcing act.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marguerite subsequently demands promises of obedience, freedom, and discretion, to which Armand separately agrees.\",\"supports\":\"The scene contains relational undertakings, but their explicit realization outside the highlighted utterance argues against assigning P to the core words merely because they occur in courtship.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense “je vous aime” in Chapter X of Alexandre Dumas fils’s La Dame aux camélias.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core occurrence and its grammatical presentation as a current love-state avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the utterance’s core force. The scene’s passion, intimacy, courtship, and later promises are adequately represented as contextual features or separate acts; no residual dimension requires E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
